{"step_index":2922,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:21Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n12. Phase 10: Testing and Validation\n\nTest:\n\nCatalog record uniqueness.\n\nTopic assignments.\n\nProblem search and filters.\n\nDifficulty filters.\n\nProblem detail pages.\n\nPython reference solutions.\n\nTest case execution.\n\nExecution visualizer integration.\n\nPrevious and Next Step behavior.\n\nProgress persistence after refresh.\n\nImport and export.\n\nBookmark and note persistence.\n\nResponsive layouts.\n\nExisting application functionality.\n\nRun regression tests against the original visualizer before and after implementation.\n\nDo not claim all 300 problems or solutions are verified unless they actually have been checked.\n\n13. Implementation Instructions\n\nBefore coding:\n\nInspect the existing repository and application architecture.\n\nIdentify the existing frontend, backend, execution engine, and storage mechanism.\n\nDetermine which components can be reused.\n\nPropose a concise implementation plan.\n\nIdentify files that need modification and new files that are necessary.\n\nThen implement the project incrementally, in the phases described above.\n\nKeep the problem catalog in structured data files or another maintainable data source rather than hardcoding problem records into UI components.\n\nKeep the problem catalog, progress model, Python solutions, and interface components separate.\n\nAvoid unnecessary dependencies and large architectural rewrites.\n\nAfter each phase, run relevant tests and fix regressions before proceeding.\n\nAt completion, report:\n\nFeatures implemented.\n\nNumber of real, unique catalog problems added.\n\nNumber of reference solutions tested.\n\nFiles created and modified.\n\nTests passed and failed.\n\nFeatures that remain incomplete.\n\nFinal Goal\n\nTurn my existing Code Flow Visualizer into a beginner-friendly DSA learning platform with 300 curated problems, Python-